  "metadata": {
    "title": "Elon Musk's trillionaire leap carries big stakes for Nashville, Memphis",
    "byline": null,
    "published_at": null,
    "site_name": "finance.yahoo.com",
    "language": null
  },
  "content": {
    "text": "The world's richest man, Elon Musk, just became richer.\nMusk was already the wealthiest man on the planet but has now become the world's first trillionaire thanks to his company, SpaceX's, foray into the public stock market.\nIt raised $75 billion from its initial public offering, when a private company sells its stock to the public for the first time, giving the company a new value of roughly $1.77 trillion.\nThat makes Musk wealthy enough to spend $1 million a day for 2,700 years. His net worth is now more than $700 billion higher than the next closest billionaire, Google co-founder Larry Page.\nHow it all ties to Nashville and Tennessee\nThe implications of Musk's staggering wealth are being felt in Nashville and across Tennessee, as Musk-tied projects reshape the environment, above and below ground.\nIn Nashville, the Musk-owned Boring Company is building the Music City Loop, a set of privately funded twin tunnels that will connect Nashville International Airport to downtown Nashville near the Tennessee Capitol building. Once complete, riders will be able to take a trip in either direction in a Tesla vehicle, tying in another of Musk's companies.\nThe company's plans eventually call for the loop to extend along Nashville's West End corridor, with stops also planned at Music City Center and at the end of Lower Broadway near the Cumberland River.\nThe company also announced plans for its first residential station agreement in early May, serving residents in a trio of downtown apartment towers. According to that announcement, residents will be able to take an elevator directly to a Music City Loop station within the building and be taken to the airport in roughly nine minutes or to Lower Broadway in roughly two minutes.\nThe project was announced nearly a year ago and was quickly met with controversy as Nashville lawmakers derided a deal between The Boring Company and the state that allowed for little local oversight, given that tunneling is planned for entirely underneath state-owned roadways.\nTunneling began in late February and has escalated since then. The company's most recent public updates about the project, announced in a June 1 blog post, note that its advanced Prufock-MB2 tunneling machine will launch this month and the even more advanced Prufock-MB3 machine is currently under construction.\nThat blog post also notes that The Boring Company has acquired all 37 permits and approvals needed to begin tunneling, with another eight related to additional launch sites required to complete the Broadway and airport alignments still in progress.",
    "text_blocks": [
      "The world's richest man, Elon Musk, just became richer.",
      "Musk was already the wealthiest man on the planet but has now become the world's first trillionaire thanks to his company, SpaceX's, foray into the public stock market.",
      "It raised $75 billion from its initial public offering, when a private company sells its stock to the public for the first time, giving the company a new value of roughly $1.77 trillion.",
      "That makes Musk wealthy enough to spend $1 million a day for 2,700 years. His net worth is now more than $700 billion higher than the next closest billionaire, Google co-founder Larry Page.",
      "How it all ties to Nashville and Tennessee",
      "The implications of Musk's staggering wealth are being felt in Nashville and across Tennessee, as Musk-tied projects reshape the environment, above and below ground.",
      "In Nashville, the Musk-owned Boring Company is building the Music City Loop, a set of privately funded twin tunnels that will connect Nashville International Airport to downtown Nashville near the Tennessee Capitol building. Once complete, riders will be able to take a trip in either direction in a Tesla vehicle, tying in another of Musk's companies.",
      "The company's plans eventually call for the loop to extend along Nashville's West End corridor, with stops also planned at Music City Center and at the end of Lower Broadway near the Cumberland River.",
      "The company also announced plans for its first residential station agreement in early May, serving residents in a trio of downtown apartment towers. According to that announcement, residents will be able to take an elevator directly to a Music City Loop station within the building and be taken to the airport in roughly nine minutes or to Lower Broadway in roughly two minutes.",
      "The project was announced nearly a year ago and was quickly met with controversy as Nashville lawmakers derided a deal between The Boring Company and the state that allowed for little local oversight, given that tunneling is planned for entirely underneath state-owned roadways.",
      "Tunneling began in late February and has escalated since then. The company's most recent public updates about the project, announced in a June 1 blog post, note that its advanced Prufock-MB2 tunneling machine will launch this month and the even more advanced Prufock-MB3 machine is currently under construction.",
      "That blog post also notes that The Boring Company has acquired all 37 permits and approvals needed to begin tunneling, with another eight related to additional launch sites required to complete the Broadway and airport alignments still in progress."
    ],
